"""Report content: chapters 6-10 + appendix of the Software Industry Gap Analysis report."""

CH6 = {
    'num': '6', 'title': 'Missing Links: Connective Tissue Never Built', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'A missing link is defined precisely: two things that both exist and both work, but are not connected. Building the connective tissue — rather than improving either endpoint — unlocks outsized value, because the endpoints already have entire industries maintaining them. Track R3 evaluated twelve candidate links and discarded four as already being built (API contract testing, design-token format interop, compliance-as-code, and local-file-to-cloud bridging). The eight survivors below share a single historical cause: the join between the two representations always cost more than the failures it prevented. That equation has now flipped.'),
        ('h2', '6.1  Design intent and shipped production code'),
        ('p', 'Figma files are the source of design truth; running production interfaces are the source of behavioral truth; and the two drift apart the day after every handoff. In Figma\'s own 2025 State of the Designer report, 91% of developers and 92% of designers agree the design-to-code handoff still needs improvement. Every existing tool is either a one-shot converter (which bridges once, then drift resumes) or screenshot-to-screenshot visual regression (which detects change, not drift from design intent). The newly buildable connector is continuous "design drift CI": render the deployed interface, diff it against the linked Figma frames at token and component granularity, and post a reviewable drift report on every pull request. The W3C Design Tokens Format Module reached a stable specification in October 2025, giving the diff a machine-readable common layer for the first time, and vision-capable models can now attribute differences to specific tokens rather than emitting a wall of pixel noise.'),
        ('h2', '6.2  Specs and tests: executable requirement traceability'),
        ('p', 'Requirements traceability survived only inside regulated-industry ALM suites priced for aerospace and medical budgets; mainstream agile teams dropped it entirely rather than pay the tax. Now spec-driven development is surging precisely because AI agents need machine-readable specifications to write code against (martinfowler.com, Oct 2025) — but the trace runs one way, spec to code. Nothing checks the return path: that every requirement has an assertion, and that every test is justified by a requirement. When agents write most of the code, the specification becomes the only human-reviewed artifact, and a "trace linter" — coverage.py for requirements instead of lines, failing CI on orphan requirements or orphan tests — becomes the audit trail for the whole pipeline. The wedge into the market is the fast-growing spec-driven-development community itself.'),
        ('h2', '6.3  Docs and runtime: self-verifying documentation'),
        ('p', 'This is the same wound as gap 4.3, seen from the link side: documentation and deployed behavior are two representations of one system with no verifier between them. Guides to documentation drift recommend discipline, not verification (mintlify.com, Jun 2026); freshness badges measure age, not incorrectness; AI doc generators snapshot behavior and then drift one generation later. The mechanical version — extract every checkable claim from a document, verify it against code, OpenAPI schemas, or a live staging environment, and file a precise "this section is now false" report naming the offending commit — only became a mechanical task with current language models. Start with READMEs, where the pain is universal and the land is open source; monetize enterprise runbooks, where being wrong is most expensive.'),
        ('h2', '6.4  AI-generated code and the verification trust layer'),
        ('callout', '96% / 48%', 'of developers do not fully trust AI-generated code (96%), yet only 48% always verify it before committing — the behavioral gap a merge-gate ledger closes (SonarSource, Jan 2026)'),
        ('p', 'Code review assumes the author understood what they wrote; machine-generated code breaks that assumption, and the safety net — linters, tests, static analysis — certifies syntax and coverage, never provenance or comprehension. The numbers describe a systemic risk rather than a preference: 96% of developers do not fully trust AI-generated code while only 48% always verify it (SonarSource, Jan 2026); usage rose to 84% while trust fell eleven points in a year to 29% (Stack Overflow 2025). No attestation standard exists for code authorship — C2PA covers media, SLSA covers build provenance, and neither answers the question a tech lead actually asks: which lines were machine-written, were they ever run, and does the human sign-off understand them. A verification ledger at the merge gate — machine-readable attestations bound to every agent-authored pull request, with a per-repository dashboard of trusted-versus-verified — is the CI/CD of the agent era, and the field is empty.'),
        ('h2', '6.5  Deploys and incidents: the missing final rung'),
        ('p', 'Ticket-to-commit-to-deploy linking exists inside individual vendors (Azure DevOps documents work-item linking; Jira plus GitHub Actions visualize deploys). The rung that would answer "which change caused the outage" — the deploy-to-incident causal join — is manual everywhere: root-cause timelines are reconstructed by hand, under stress, after the fact, which is why postmortems read like autopsies (hyperping.com, Apr 2026). The four data types live in four vendors, each pairwise integration is shallow, and correlating deploys to regressions requires statistical joinery nobody wanted to own. CDEvents and OpenTelemetry now normalize deploy events, and warehouse-native analytics makes the cross-vendor join trivial — a "change ledger" that auto-correlates and auto-drafts the suspected-change section of every postmortem sells directly to the incident commander.'),
        ('h2', '6.6  Feature flags, analytics, and code: the retirement ledger'),
        ('p', 'Stale flags are quantified technical debt — more than $125,000 per organization per year (flagshark.com, Jun 2025) — and every flag vendor now publishes a flag-debt guide, which means the debt is endemic and self-inflicted. Flag platforms and analytics platforms remain two systems joined manually per experiment: nothing ties a flag\'s decision to the business number that justifies keeping or killing it, because flag evaluation lives in application runtime while outcome data lives in the warehouse, and nothing stamps events with the flag state that produced them. The buildable shim stamps all telemetry events with active flag states, then builds the automatic ledger — flag to code paths to outcomes to a retirement recommendation ("kill flag X: saves three thousand lines, no metric moved in ninety days").'),
        ('h2', '6.7  CRM context and engineering systems: tenant observability'),
        ('p', 'The support-escalation ritual is pure waste: a ticket opens, a support engineer exchanges messages trying to reproduce an issue (leaddev.com, Feb 2026), while the specific facts — which version this tenant runs, which flags are active for them, which recent deploy touched their workflow, which errors their requests produced — sit in engineering systems one join key away. The join key (tenant identifier) exists on both sides and was never propagated through telemetry. Multi-tenant SaaS is now the default and OpenTelemetry supports tenant attributes, so the missing link is a middleware that captures per-account runtime snapshots and injects them into Zendesk or Intercom tickets automatically — observability aimed at the support queue instead of the dashboard.'),
        ('h2', '6.8  Formal methods and mainstream development'),
        ('p', 'Formal verification is mature, proven, and fringe — kept out of the mainstream by the one cost that mattered: writing formal properties cost more engineer-time than the bugs it prevented. Martin Kleppmann\'s December 2025 argument is that AI collapses exactly that translation cost. The mainstreamable subset does not require theorem proving: a "property copilot" that reads a pull request, proposes a handful of prose-derived invariants ("money is never created or destroyed in a transfer") as runtime monitors, executes them in staging and production, and flags violations with the trace. Runtime verification as a service is the property gap between what tests check and what types guarantee — and it is now fillable.'),
        ('p', 'Discarded as already building, for the record: API contract testing (a mature ecosystem and a reported $2.8B services market in 2025); design-token format interop (the W3C stable format plus Style Dictionary closed it); compliance-as-code (Drata, RegScale, and Ketryx already sell it); and local-files-to-cloud bridging (real pain, but fragmented with no small-team wedge).'),
    ],
}

CH7 = {
    'num': '7', 'title': 'Badly-Built Categories: Why Hated Software Stays Bad', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'Some categories have hosted hated software for ten to twenty years while dozens of well-funded challengers failed to fix them. Track R4\'s core finding is that this is not an accident of execution but a set of compounding structural mechanisms — and that AI changes the economics of exactly one of them. Understanding which mechanism locks a category tells you whether a genuine fix is even possible, and what shape its distribution must take.'),
        ('h2', '7.1  The four mechanisms'),
        ('bullets', [
            '<b>Buyer does not equal user.</b> The person selecting the software (IT, procurement, a VP) is not the person who suffers daily: "a bad UI doesn\'t impact sales, because the people who do the purchase don\'t use the application" (Reddit r/sysadmin, Dec 2025). Enterprise incentives are "entirely misaligned" (Medium, Jul 2026) — an observation HN was making in 2010 that remains true in 2025.',
            '<b>The software faithfully encodes a resented process.</b> "My biggest problem is not Jira itself, but how people are using it" (Ministry of Testing, Feb 2025); project-management tools "fail because of resistance, not because of missing features" (Breeze.pm, Oct 2025). Automating a hated process produces hated software, whatever the UX.',
            '<b>"Good enough" plus integration moats.</b> Legacy tools sit on SSO, workflows, exports, and muscle memory; enterprise systems historically carried data error rates above 50% and survived anyway, because replacing them is riskier than hating them (MIT Sloan Review, 2007, still cited).',
            '<b>Checklist procurement has no UX feedback loop.</b> Buying is checklist-driven, so vendors invest in the demo-to-checkout path, not the daily-use path; poor user adoption causes roughly 70% of digital-transformation initiatives to fail (UX Matters, Feb 2025).',
        ]),
        ('p', 'The 2025-26 AI twist: for the first time the marginal cost of manual entry, summarization, and retrieval is collapsing — service-desk-class agents report up to 60% reductions in manual workload (BCG, Oct 2025). That attacks the data-quality half of the moats and the manual-entry layer of the hatred, but it does <b>not</b> fix the buyer-user mismatch, which is organizational rather than technical. The table summarizes which mechanisms AI unlocks.'),
        ('table', {
            'title': 'Table 2. Which lock-in mechanisms does the AI era actually unlock?',
            'header': ['Mechanism', 'Representative locked categories', 'Does AI (2025-26) unlock it?'],
            'rows': [
                ['Buyer is not the user', 'CRM, project management, intranets, expense', 'No — requires bottom-up distribution'],
                ['Encodes a hated process', 'Project management, approvals, timesheets', 'Partially — agents can bypass process steps'],
                ['Manual entry and retrieval cost', 'CRM, notes, timesheets, docs, search', 'Yes — this is the big unlock'],
                ['Integration and permission moats', 'Search, email, intranets, PM tools', 'Partially — the connector layer is the new moat'],
                ['"Good enough" trap and free defaults', 'Email, spreadsheets', 'Yes — via outcome pricing instead of seat pricing'],
            ],
            'ratios': [0.28, 0.38, 0.34],
        }),
        ('h2', '7.2  CRM: the cleanest case in the industry'),
        ('p', 'Salespeople spend roughly 60% of the week on non-selling activity (Attention, citing Salesforce\'s own numbers, Feb 2025), and 79% of the opportunity data reps collect never makes it into the CRM (DevRev, 2026). The rep\'s labor is the input; the manager\'s dashboard is the output; the rep receives zero personal return for typing — so they rationally stop typing, and CRM adoption failure rates of 20-70% follow (heydan.ai, Dec 2025). A decade of mobile apps, gamification, and mandatory-field policies treated the symptom. The genuine fix newly buildable: CRM as an observation system rather than an entry system — calls become transcripts become structured fields, with the rep reviewing rather than typing, sold rep-first so adoption is voluntary.'),
        ('h2', '7.3  The expense-report dead zone'),
        ('p', '"We hate them. The companies that build expense management software know that we hate them" (New York Times, Nov 2024). Half of workers do not file some reports at all, abandoning an average of $26.25 each (WalkMe, Dec 2023); a single report still costs $58 to process and 19% contain errors (Corpay, Mar 2026). Corporate cards and OCR attacked the receipt; the residual pain is policy, out-of-pocket float, and multi-step approvals — org-process pain that software mirrors faithfully. The credible fix kills the report rather than improving it: card-first everywhere plus agents that assemble evidence and auto-approve in-policy spend, with finance buying exception-handling instead of review-of-everything.'),
        ('h2', '7.4  Meetings: transcription is solved, execution is not'),
        ('p', 'Unproductive meetings cost businesses on the order of $375B annually (Fellow.ai, May 2025), and individual contributors now lose 3.7 hours per week to them, up 118% since 2019 (Asana data, via SpeakWise, 2026). AI notetakers finally crossed the adoption line — roughly 75% of professionals use one (Saner.ai, Jul 2026) — yet "the action items they extract stay yours to carry out, and each meeting becomes its own silo" (Tana, Jul 2026). The unbuilt layer is the write-back: notes that flow into tickets, CRM, and documents automatically, with consent tooling for the new legal exposure the transcripts create (Mayer Brown, Jun 2026). Better transcripts are a commodity; the meeting-to-action loop is the product.'),
        ('h2', '7.5  Email: a graveyard that teaches the real lesson'),
        ('p', 'The email-client category is a museum of failed "fixes": Notion Mail shut down in September 2026 roughly a year after launch, discontinued in favor of AI agents (TechCrunch, Jun 2026), with more than half of its users never actually opening the inbox (Enterprisedna, Jun 2026); Superhuman, the celebrated UX play, ended up absorbed into Grammarly. The 2026 lesson generalizes to every category in this chapter: people do not want a faster version of the hated thing — they want less of the thing. Notion\'s own pivot is the tell: agents that drain the inbox (draft, schedule, extract tasks, unsubscribe) rather than clients that render it more beautifully.'),
        ('h2', '7.6  Intranets, docs, and timesheets: the same shape'),
        ('p', 'Fifty-seven percent of employees see no purpose in their company intranet (Firstup, Jun 2024) and up to 90% fail to deliver clear value (Coveo, via Nexinite, Aug 2025) — two masters, billboard and tool, serving neither. Documentation rot was quantified in Chapter 4; the wedge is generation from the work itself plus staleness detection. Time tracking punishes its user while benefiting the firm\'s billing, and costs law firms $25,000-$100,000 per year in unbilled time (LeanLaw) — passive reconstruction of the day from calendar, email, and documents, presented as a draft timesheet to edit, is the fix now that reconstruction quality crossed the usability threshold. Every one of these wedges has the same shape: sell the suffering user the tool that removes their work; sell the resulting clean data to the buyer.'),
    ],
}

CH8 = {
    'num': '8', 'title': 'Frontier Gaps: AI Infrastructure and Unsexy Industries', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'The frontier lens covers two very different territories: the plumbing that the AI application era is missing underneath itself, and the industries where the software industry never bothered to show up. The screening discipline matters most here, because the AI-infrastructure space moves fastest: LLM tracing was discarded as solved (nine or more mature competitors), prompt versioning was discarded (instant rollback is now standard), and local inference operations was deprioritized as commoditized by the vLLM and Ollama ecosystems. What survived is narrower and sharper.'),
        ('h2', '8.1  Evals-in-CI: testing non-deterministic systems where engineers already live'),
        ('p', 'Every AI feature currently ships with effectively no meaningful test coverage, because pytest asserts equality and model outputs are non-deterministic — "part of your code now returns a different answer every time you run it" (kig.re, Jun 2026). Existing eval platforms run offline datasets in their own dashboards, a manually-triggered world outside the pipeline engineers already trust, while flaky evals "wreck your CI signal" (BuildPulse, Jul 2026). The stakes are escalating fast, as the chart below shows: documented AI incidents rose from 233 in 2024 to 362 in 2025, and enterprise LLM API spend more than doubled from $3.5B to $8.4B in roughly six months. The missing product is a GitHub-Action-shaped statistical gate — fixed seeds, pass-at-k thresholds, regression bands against stored baselines — that makes "npm test" meaningful again when the model is stochastic. Distribution is native developer tooling: no sales force required.'),
        ('chart', '/home/z/my-project/scripts/assets/chart2_escalation.png',
         'Figure 2. The AI era is escalating faster than its tooling: documented AI incidents (left) and enterprise LLM API spend (right)'),
        ('h2', '8.2  Portable agent memory'),
        ('p', 'Agent memory is becoming the most valuable user data since email, and it is trapped per vendor: "Mem0\'s memory is stored in their cloud infrastructure, creating a new form of vendor lock-in" (arXiv, May 2026). Open standards cover tool calls (MCP) and agent-to-agent messaging (A2A) but not persistent user memory; every chat history is already fragmented across providers with no interop. An open-source, local-first memory server with a documented export format and a thin hosted sync tier is a standards play — credibility-first, slow to monetize, and strategically positioned for the moment regulation turns memory portability into the next browser-data fight.'),
        ('h2', '8.3  AI FinOps: cost allocation, not token counters'),
        ('p', 'Gateways count tokens and observability platforms graph spend, but engineering still cannot answer "what did this feature cost us per customer this month?" — and agent loops make cost non-linear, since one bad prompt can burn a month of budget. With CFOs now seeing an AI line item and asking for chargeback, the missing layer is FinOps-style allocation: tagging spend to feature, team, and customer; amortizing compounding agent-loop costs; and a policy engine that auto-downgrades or halts runaway agents. An open-source allocation and guardrail engine sitting on data teams already emit (gateways, OpenTelemetry) lands via the OSS route and expands into hosted policy enforcement.'),
        ('h2', '8.4  Unsexy industries: construction sub-tier and freight brokerage'),
        ('p', 'Twenty-seven percent of AECO professionals still rely on Excel and PDFs (PR Newswire, Jul 2025), and 85% of construction professionals use Excel for estimating and costing (premiercs.com, 2026) — while the existing tools target large general contractors at enterprise pricing, leaving the five-to-fifty-person subcontractors who actually bid the work unserved. Camera takeoff and email-to-estimate workflows newly remove the data-entry barrier that made sub-tier software uneconomical; the channel is trade associations rather than a field sales force. Freight brokerage is the parallel case: brokerages run on thirty-year-old TMS plus phone-email-Excel glue, and the actual money — per-lane margin visibility, carrier-risk scoring fed by live data — is tribal knowledge an overlay can now read from broker inboxes and TMS exports directly.'),
        ('table', {
            'title': 'Table 3. Shippability of the frontier gaps for a 2-4 person team',
            'header': ['Gap', 'Sales force needed?', 'Why it wins for a small team'],
            'rows': [
                ['Evals-in-CI adapter', 'No — developer PLG', 'Rides existing CI rails; deterministic-statistics wedge; OSS to hosted'],
                ['AI FinOps allocation', 'No — OSS + PLG', 'Sits on data teams already emit; policy engine is the moat'],
                ['Code provenance attestation', 'No — compliance buyers, inbound', 'Standards-shaped greenfield; regulation tailwind'],
                ['Portable agent memory', 'No, but slow monetization', 'Standards play; credibility first; pair with consulting'],
                ['Construction sub-tier estimating', 'Eventually — association channel first', 'LLM takeoff removes the data-entry barrier; one trade, one region'],
                ['Freight margin intelligence', 'Phone-sales motion, no field force', 'Overlay-not-replacement avoids the TMS sales cycle'],
            ],
            'ratios': [0.28, 0.30, 0.42],
        }),
        ('p', 'Honesty about coverage: field services, small-manufacturer MES, government legacy systems, elder care, and SMB supply-chain visibility were queued in the research plan but could not be source-verified within this pass due to search-quota rate limits. Prior expectation is that they are structurally similar to the two validated verticals — spreadsheet shadow systems below an enterprise pricing floor — but they are flagged as follow-up research rather than asserted as findings.'),
    ],
}

CH9 = {
    'num': '9', 'title': 'Cross-Cutting Patterns: Why These Gaps Persist', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'When forty-odd validated gaps from five independent tracks are laid side by side, six structural patterns explain nearly all of them. These patterns are the actual research finding — the individual gaps are instances. They also explain why the gaps are opening now rather than ten years ago, and why a small team can win several of them against incumbents who cannot respond.'),
        ('h2', '9.1  The ownership vacuum'),
        ('p', 'The single most repeated failure mode across all tracks: a layer works, and nobody is paid to watch it. Integrations break silently because the iPaaS monetizes task volume, not reliability; documentation rots because no test suite goes red; permission graphs drift because identity syncs accounts rather than access; the deploy-to-incident join is manual because the four data types live in four vendors, each with shallow pairwise integrations. Every one of these is a monitoring or verification layer sold to the person who gets paged — a buyer who feels the pain weekly and can approve a small purchase without a committee.'),
        ('h2', '9.2  Every layer presumes an operator'),
        ('p', 'dbt presumes an analytics engineer; Kafka presumes a platform team; SailPoint presumes an IT department; mainframe modernization presumes a consultancy. Below roughly one hundred employees those people do not exist, so the layer silently reverts to spreadsheets, email attachments, and nightly SFTP drops — the "actual data layer" of the world economy. The products that win the SMB tier will be the ones that package the operator\'s judgment as defaults, not the ones that offer the operator\'s tools at a lower price.'),
        ('h2', '9.3  Entry is funded, exit is not'),
        ('p', 'Every vendor builds generous import wizards; none productizes the exit. The result is a $315,000 average cost per migration, a 62% data-migration failure driver for ERP projects, and a regulations-versus-tooling gap now opening under the EU Data Act. Migration-as-a-service for specific fleeing paths is structurally under-supplied because exit buyers are one-time and awkward — which is exactly why the niche remains empty and why referral deals with destination vendors can substitute for a recurring sales motion.'),
        ('h2', '9.4  The translation-cost collapse'),
        ('p', 'The meta-pattern of Chapter 6: nearly every durable missing link — design to code, spec to test, prose to formal property, ticket to context, workbook to schema — stayed unbuilt because translating between two representations cost more engineer-time than the failures it prevented. Language models collapse precisely that cost. This is the "why now" for half the gaps in this report, and it is also a screening heuristic for any additional gap candidate: if the blocker was translation between representations, it is now buildable; if the blocker was a buyer-user mismatch or a distribution problem, AI does not help.'),
        ('h2', '9.5  Buyer-user mismatch survives the AI era'),
        ('p', 'Chapter 7\'s deepest lesson deserves repetition at the pattern level, because it is the one AI does not fix. Categories where the suffering user has no purchasing power and the vendor\'s revenue depends on the pain\'s byproduct — data, control, compliance — regenerate their badness through every technology cycle. The 2026 distribution answer is bottom-up: give the rep, the developer, the support agent a tool that removes their unpaid work, and sell the clean exhaust data upward. Notion Mail\'s shutdown is the proof that "a better version of the hated thing" loses to "the hated thing, deleted."'),
        ('h2', '9.6  Overlay beats replacement for small teams'),
        ('p', 'The gaps a two-to-four person team can actually win are overlays on incumbents rather than replacements of them: the evals adapter rides GitHub Actions rather than competing with eval platforms; the AI-FinOps engine sits on gateway and OpenTelemetry data rather than replacing the gateway; the freight margin overlay reads the TMS rather than replacing it; the integration observability proxy watches the wiring rather than rewiring it. Replacement sales cycles need field forces and years; overlay products sell to the person in pain, in a self-serve motion, while the incumbent cannot retaliate without cannibalizing its own platform revenue.'),
    ],
}

CH10 = {
    'num': '10', 'title': 'The Build Shortlist: What a Small Team Could Ship', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'This chapter converts the research into a ranked shortlist. The ranking weighs four factors: strength of the evidence that the gap is real; openness of the field (no dominant solution within reach); whether the enabling shift has actually happened rather than being promised; and realism of the distribution model for a team of two to four people with no field sales force. Twelve opportunities cleared the bar. The top three deserve detail before the table.'),
        ('h2', '10.1  First: the evals-in-CI adapter'),
        ('p', 'It addresses the sharpest unmet need found in the entire research — every AI feature shipping with no meaningful test coverage, inside a market whose incident count and spend are both doubling (Figure 2) — and it has a native developer distribution path. The product is narrow on purpose: a CI component that wraps existing eval suites in deterministic statistics (fixed seeds, pass-at-k thresholds, regression bands against stored baselines) and posts an ordinary green-or-red check on the pull request. It does not compete with eval platforms; it connects them to the workflow engineers already trust. That narrowness is the moat: the big platforms monetize dashboards and judge-token spend, so none of them is structurally motivated to make the CI adapter excellent.'),
        ('h2', '10.2  Second: the verification ledger for AI-generated code'),
        ('p', 'The 96-versus-48 trust gap is a quantified, growing tax on every engineering organization, and no attestation standard exists for code authorship — the field is empty at exactly the moment regulation (the EU AI Act phase-in and secure-software attestation momentum) begins demanding provenance records for machine-generated artifacts. The buildable first version is a Git attestation layer: per-commit metadata recording AI-assistance level, binding to the tests that actually ran, and an "understood-by" sign-off, surfaced as a pull-request badge and an exportable audit report. Landing via an open-source CLI makes adoption voluntary and viral inside engineering orgs; the compliance buyer arrives later and pays enterprise prices.'),
        ('h2', '10.3  Third: the integration observability proxy'),
        ('p', 'Chapter 5\'s ownership vacuum in product form: a read-only proxy that sits in front of the customer\'s existing Zapier wiring, webhooks, and exports, alerts on schema drift, empty payloads, and dropped events, and offers one-click replay with diff reports. It requires no migration, sells to whoever gets paged when the sync dies, and rides the agent-integration wave (under 5% to a projected 40% of enterprise apps within 2026) that is about to multiply the amount of unwatched wiring in the world. The wedge is deliberately unglamorous — which is why no incumbent competes for it.'),
        ('table', {
            'title': 'Table 4. Twelve ranked opportunities from the research',
            'header': ['#', 'Opportunity', 'Lens', 'Why the field is open', 'First ship'],
            'rows': [
                ['1', 'Evals-in-CI adapter', 'AI infra', 'Eval platforms monetize dashboards, not the CI gate; no agreed green/red semantics exist', 'GitHub Action wrapping existing eval suites'],
                ['2', 'AI-code verification ledger', 'Links', 'No attestation standard for code authorship; regulation creating demand', 'OSS CLI + PR badge'],
                ['3', 'Integration observability proxy', 'Plumbing', 'iPaaS monetizes volume, not reliability; nobody watches post-setup', 'Read-only proxy with replay'],
                ['4', 'Self-verifying docs (doc tests)', 'Tooling/Links', 'Freshness tools measure age, not truth; RAG made rot worse', 'README checker, OSS-first'],
                ['5', 'Flaky-test root-cause repair', 'Tooling', 'Everyone detects and retries; nobody diagnoses or fixes', 'CI plugin filing diagnosis PRs'],
                ['6', 'CRM as observation system', 'Categories', '79% of data never entered; entry-system model is structurally dead', 'Call capture to structured fields, rep-first'],
                ['7', 'AI FinOps allocation and guardrails', 'AI infra', 'Token counters everywhere; allocation and runaway-agent policy nowhere', 'OSS engine on gateway/OTel data'],
                ['8', 'Permission census scanner', 'Plumbing', 'SCIM is enterprise-gated; permission graphs invisible', 'Headless-browser audit crawler'],
                ['9', 'Spec-to-test trace linter', 'Links', 'SDD surge is one-directional; return path unchecked', 'GitHub Action for SDD repos'],
                ['10', 'Flat-priced small-team observability', 'Tooling', 'Enterprise pricing punishes small teams; 30+ alternatives, no winner', 'One binary, one price, OTel-native'],
                ['11', 'Construction sub-tier estimating', 'Unsexy', 'Tools priced for GCs; 85% of estimating still in Excel', 'One trade, camera takeoff, association channel'],
                ['12', 'Excel absorption layer', 'Plumbing', 'Killers demand rebuilds; nobody absorbs the workbook', 'Schema + audit log around the live file'],
            ],
            'ratios': [0.045, 0.235, 0.115, 0.38, 0.225],
        }),
        ('p', 'Two disciplines should govern any entry into this list. First, verify the gap again at build time with fresh searches — the AI-infrastructure rows move fastest, and a category that was open in early 2026 may be closing by late 2026. Second, choose the overlay shape wherever one exists: the pattern across all five tracks is that small teams win by watching, verifying, or connecting systems that incumbents built and then neglected — not by asking anyone to rip anything out. The gaps are real, quantified, and structural; the scarce resource is the willingness to build unglamorous plumbing in markets where nobody else is paid to care.'),
    ],
}

